.TH "HTMLMEDIAELEMENT.GETSTARTDATE" "3JS" "March 23, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
HTMLMediaElement.getStartDate \- HTMLMediaElement: getStartDate() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]getStartDate()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]HTMLMediaElement\f[R] interface returns a new \f[CR]Date\f[R]
object representing the real\-world date and time corresponding to the
beginning of the media.
.PP
This is useful for media streams that are anchored to a real\-world
clock, such as a live broadcast that began at a specific date and time.
For media that does not include date and time information, the returned
\f[CR]Date\f[R] object will have a time value of \f[CR]NaN\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
getStartDate()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]Date\f[R] object representing the start date and time of the
media.
If the media does not include date and time information, the returned
\f[CR]Date\f[R] object will have a time value of \f[CR]NaN\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
Internally, each media element tracks a start date, which begins as
\f[CR]NaN\f[R] (not set).
Once the browser has loaded enough data to read the media\(cqs metadata,
it sets the start date to the real\-world time that corresponds to the
beginning of the media \(em if the format provides one.
If it doesn\(cqt, the start date stays \f[CR]NaN\f[R].
.PP
For media that does specify a start time and date (for example, a live
TV broadcast streamed over the web), \f[CR]getStartDate()\f[R] returns a
\f[CR]Date\f[R] object corresponding to the real\-world time at which
the media begins.
This allows media player controls to display absolute times (such as
\(lq2:30 PM\(rq) rather than times relative to the start of playback
(such as \(lq3 hours, 12 minutes\(rq).
.PP
The returned \f[CR]Date\f[R] will have a time value of \f[CR]NaN\f[R]
(making it an invalid date) in either of the following cases:
.IP \(bu 2
No data has been loaded yet (\f[CR]readyState\f[R] is
\f[CR]HAVE_NOTHING\f[R]), so the start date hasn\(cqt been set.
.IP \(bu 2
The media format doesn\(cqt include date and time information.
.PP
The start date is not guaranteed to be available as soon as the
\f[CR]loadedmetadata\f[R] event fires.
For example, \c
.UR https://developer.apple.com/documentation/http-live-streaming
HLS
.UE \c
\ streams carry dates in segment\-level
\f[CR]#EXT\-X\-PROGRAM\-DATE\-TIME\f[R] tags, which may not have been
read yet at that point.
Listening for the \f[CR]loadeddata\f[R] event instead is more reliable
across formats, because by then the browser has loaded enough data to
determine the start date.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Displaying the start date of a live stream
This example retrieves the start date of a live stream \(em the
real\-world date and time at which the broadcast began, as embedded in
the stream by the server \(em and displays it.
It listens for the \f[CR]loadeddata\f[R] event, which fires once enough
data has been loaded for the start date to be available.
.SS HTML
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]video\f[R] src=\(dqlivestream.m3u8\(dq controls></\f[B]video\f[R]>
<\f[B]output\f[R]>Start date: loading\&...</\f[B]output\f[R]>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] video = document.querySelector(\(dqvideo\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] display = document.querySelector(\(dqoutput\(dq);

video.addEventListener(\(dqloadeddata\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]const\f[R] startDate = video.getStartDate();

  \f[B]if\f[R] (isNaN(startDate.getTime())) {
    display.textContent = \(dqStart date: not available\(dq;
  } \f[B]else\f[R] {
    display.textContent = \(gaStart date: ${startDate.toLocaleString()}\(ga;
  }
});
.EE
.SS Result
The output below shows the start date of the media, as provided by the
server.
Note that this is encoded in the example metadata in \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/blob/main/media/getstartdate/stream.m3u8
stream.m3u8
.UE \c
\&.
